{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And YHWH opened.</b> Our Sages, may their memory be blessed, held that ten things were created on the eve of Sabbath at twilight. In my view, the reason is that HaShem decreed to innovate signs through these [phenomena], which are outside the natural order. The Gaon [Saadia] said that the she-ass did not speak, and R. Samuel ben Hofni adopted this. R. Samuel the Spaniard, the author of the poems, sought to rescue the one caught [i.e., to defend the literal reading]. Know that men of equal intellect found it necessary to remove the matter from its plain sense, for they estimated that HaShem would not innovate a sign in the world to change the order He created, except to vindicate His prophet. But they did not speak truth, for Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah had a sign performed for them and they were not prophets. Some of them say that Balaam was a prophet, but the truth is that his prophecy was for the sake of Israel's honor, for he was a soothsayer, and Scripture called him as much. Some say that he knew the science of the supernal beings, to draw their power below into shapes, and this is the meaning of 'and the enchanter is enchanted' [Numbers 23:23]. The correct view in my eyes is that he knew the constellations, and when he saw in the constellation of any person that the time of his evil had arrived, he would curse him. And when the evil came upon the cursed one, the onlookers and hearers present would suppose that because of his curse the evil came. And the proof is that he spoke deceitfully with the officers of Balak. And this is the meaning of 'I cannot transgress the word of YHWH my Elohim' — for there is no power in the created being to change the deed of the Creator, or His decree. And the sod is that the part does not change the part; rather the decrees of the All change the decree of the part. I cannot reveal this sod, for it is deep. The straightforward reading is that the she-ass spoke. If you understand the sod of the angels of Abraham and of Jacob, then you will understand the truth." "And the meaning of <b>with a stick.</b> For at the first and second times he struck her with wood or with leather." "<b>Feet.</b> I have explained it."], :book :numbers, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "Resolved הגאון as the Gaon [Saadia] and רב שמואל הספרדי בעל השירים as R. Samuel the Spaniard, the author of the poems, per standard identification with Samuel ibn Naghrila."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:19:06.383188495Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}